# pixi
.pixi/

# M13 Phase 3 (#109): originally other-platform native libs gathered here before a multi-RID
# `pixi run pack` (moot since #1474's managed port -- there is no native lib to gather); now also
# holds `pixi run coverage`'s report output.
/artifacts/

# Claude Code local subagent worktrees (isolation: worktree). Narrowly scoped so shared config
# under .claude/ (agents/, commands/, settings.json) stays committable without `git add -f`.
.claude/worktrees/


# Vendor documentation survey output (#527): a mirrored copy of both vendors public docs plus
# the derived index. Regenerate with `pixi run vendor-survey`; never commit ~7MB of vendor text.
.vendor-survey/

# ...except the derived index itself, which is 19KB rather than 7MB and is the one file
# `audit-completeness` reads (#589). Its own header states its purpose -- "EVERY page with a
# disposition, so coverage is checkable not asserted" -- and that is exactly what CI does with it;
# without it committed, the audit cannot run anywhere but a machine that has already run the survey.
#
# It is a SNAPSHOT: re-running `pixi run vendor-survey` regenerates it, and the regenerated file must
# be committed with that change or CI validates a stale index while the real corpus has moved.
# Nothing flags that today -- tracked in #620 alongside the rest of the check-that-a-check-runs work.
!.vendor-survey/ledger.tsv

# `baton dispatch` per-run room dirs (artifacts, redirected .gemini_home, flow log) -- transient
# per-invocation working state, never the deliverable.
#
# `/.baton/*` rather than `/.baton/`, because git does not descend into an ignored DIRECTORY and a
# `!` negation under one is never reached -- and `.baton/verify` below has to be tracked. It is the
# one committed file in here: the engine's own verify declaration (#1958, spec/baton.md §3), which
# only takes effect from the REVIEWED tree, so an untracked copy would grade nothing.
/.baton/*
!/.baton/verify

# #1487: the vendor-drift grace-window clock (tools/Baton.VendorProbe/DriftGrace.cs). Machine-local
# by design -- it times how long *this machine's* installed CLI has sat drifted from the committed
# pins, so committing it would either share one machine's clock with every other, or make every
# clone re-fight over it. Sits beside the tracked docs/vendor-probe.lock.json it is not.
docs/vendor-probe.drift.local.json

# Python bytecode. Four .pyc files were tracked, so every tool run dirtied the tree.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Session-local gates captures — never evidence, never committed (a stale one shipped as a false gates record once)
gates-run.txt
gates-output.txt
gates-exit.txt

# tools/fleet-glass (#1413): machine-local secrets and state, never committed. secrets.local.json
# and pusher.config.json carry the mailbox's push token / read segment; secretpatterns.local.txt is
# the secret-gate's denylist, sensitive in its own right (it is close to a map of what to grep for);
# push-state.local.json and pusher.log are per-machine run state/log. Each ships a *.example.* or
# *-allowlist-style sibling with placeholders -- see tools/fleet-glass/pusher.py's docstring.
tools/fleet-glass/secrets.local.json
tools/fleet-glass/pusher.config.json
tools/fleet-glass/secretpatterns.local.txt
tools/fleet-glass/push-state.local.json
tools/fleet-glass/ntfy-state.local.json
tools/fleet-glass/pusher.log
tools/fleet-glass/login.log
tools/fleet-glass/.wrangler/
